;;; A rule's `:match` splits a name that starts with `and` — M1e2 **W22**,
;;; register row `W22/kw-and-in-match` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked
;;; by S1e2.2.22 (2026-11-05) from the inventory of 2026-11-05's F1, on the
;;; user's ruling of that day (Q-M1e2.16).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two facts, `(p a)` and `(andz b)`, and one rule, `r`, concluding `(hit
;;; ?x)` from the premise `(andz ?x)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A keyword is a whole word: `00_ebnf.md` § 1, fact 1, excludes a reserved
;;; word from `SYMBOL` only where a word boundary follows it (`neq` is not a
;;; `SYMBOL`; `neq_test` is), so `andz` is a name like any other. `r` fires on
;;; `(andz b)`, so root holds `(hit b)`. The blind enumerator proposes `(andz
;;; a)`, `(hit a)` and `(p b)`, and R is six states, the answer of
;;; `kw-and-in-match-twin.ein`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.22 the parser read a reserved word as a prefix of a name, and
;;; this program answered wrong. The parser read the premise `(andz ?x)` as
;;; the conjunction `(and z ?x)`, of the atom `z` and the variable `?x`, which
;;; bound nothing: every command that saturated stopped at `r`, exit 1, with
;;; *unbound var ?x in :assert — bindings: {}* (`ein saturate`: prefixed
;;; `KeyError:`). (`ein solve FILE` at `35186bc0`, release and debug; every
;;; mode and `ein oracle` the same.) Since then a keyword is a whole word, at
;;; the boundary `00_ebnf.md` § 1 states (the user's ruling of 2026-11-05 on
;;; Q-M1e2.17), and the program answers its twin's R.

(relation p Node) (relation andz Node) (relation hit Node)
(p a) (andz b)
(rule r () :match (andz ?x) :assert (hit ?x))
